    "originalMediaType": "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document",
    "originalFileAvailable": true,
    "originalByteLength": 32440,
    "sanitizedHtml": "<article class=\"docx-projection\"><p><strong>DFU HBOT Burden CKS</strong><strong> Summary</strong></p><p><strong>Purpose. </strong>This DFU-specific planning CKS estimates the execution burden of a contemplated outpatient HBOT course. It combines a fixed population-level regimen envelope with one selected facility, patient-reported travel, and reported weekday-attendance difficulty. It does not determine HBOT eligibility, prescribe a regimen, predict completion or benefit, or calculate financial burden.</p><p><strong>FIXED REGIMEN RANGE  +  CONFIRMED 4-FIELD RESPONSE  →  BURDEN ANALYSIS</strong></p><h1>Three connected capabilities</h1><p><strong>1. Regimen Range. </strong>Returns both governed planning scenarios: 30 episodes and 40 episodes, five sessions per week (6 and 8 weeks), with one fixed three-hour scheduled facility block per episode. Neither scenario is selected or tailored for the patient.</p><p><strong>2. Burden Questionnaire Logic. </strong>In fixed order, collects and confirms: HBOT location from the bound U.S. roster; usual one-way miles; usual one-way travel minutes; and expected non-travel weekday-attendance difficulty (none, some, or major). Abandonment before confirmation emits no response.</p><p><strong>3. Burden Response Analysis. </strong>Validates the complete regimen and questionnaire artifacts, resolves the selected roster record, applies the same patient response to both scenarios, and returns objective burden, the reported attendance value, and one rule-derived execution-burden level.</p><h1>What is calculated—and how</h1><p><strong>Travel basis. </strong>Round-trip miles D = 2 × one-way miles. Round-trip travel hours T = (2 × one-way minutes) ÷ 60. The CKS does not geocode, route, infer speed, or adjust for traffic, parking, waiting, or weather.</p><p><strong>Each scenario (V = 30 or 40 episodes). </strong>Course weeks = V ÷ 5; course miles = V × D; travel hours = V × T; facility hours = V × 3; and total patient hours = V × (T + 3). The overall range uses the shorter scenario as its lower endpoint and the longer scenario as its upper endpoint. The additional 10 episodes also produce an explicit incremental-burden sensitivity result.</p><p><strong>Bounded presentation. </strong>Hour endpoints are rounded outward so the serialized shorter-to-longer result remains a planning bound. Travel-band and level classification always use the unrounded T, preventing display rounding from changing the category.</p><h1>Five-level execution-burden calculation</h1><p><strong>Travel band from unrounded round-trip time: </strong>low &lt;1 h; moderate 1–&lt;2 h; high 2–&lt;3 h; very high ≥3 h. The matrix combines that band with the reported attendance value; attendance does not change regimen arithmetic.</p><table><tbody><tr><th><p><strong>Travel band</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Attendance: none</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Attendance: some</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Attendance: major</strong></p></th></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Low</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>1  Low</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>2  Moderate</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>4  Very high</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Moderate</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>2  Moderate</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>3  High</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>4  Very high</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>High</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>3  High</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>4  Very high</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>5  Extreme</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Very high</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>4  Very high</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>5  Extreme</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>5  Extreme</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><h1>What is returned</h1><p><strong>Fixed-shape analysis result. </strong>The completed result keeps three concepts separate: (1) objective burden for both scenarios and the overall range; (2) weekday-attendance feasibility reproduced verbatim; and (3) the ordinal level 1–5 with primary and secondary drivers. The level is a transparent planning convention—not a clinical severity score, adherence prediction, probability, or validated psychometric measure.</p></article>",
